.TH "ZYPPER-DECLARATIVE" "1" "June 2026" "zypper-declarative 0.6.9" ""
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
zypper-declarative - declarative convergence of SUSE system state inside
a snapshot transaction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]zypper declarative\f[R] \f[I]verb\f[R]
[\f[I]key\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R] \&...]
.PP
\f[B]zypper-declarative\f[R] \f[I]verb\f[R]
[\f[I]key\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R] \&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]zypper-declarative\f[R] converges a SUSE system (SL Micro 6.2, SLES
16.1) to a declarative manifest describing the declarable subset of the
SUSE Machinery system description: \f[B]packages\f[R],
\f[B]repositories\f[R], \f[B]services\f[R], and \f[B]config_files\f[R]
(files under \f[I]/etc\f[R]).
The manifest\[cq]s canonical serialisation is JSON (Machinery
\f[I]format_version\f[R] 1); YAML is an opt-in serialisation of the same
data model.
.PP
The tool reads the live system into this same model itself (see
\f[B]describe\f[R]), so no separate collector program is required.
Convergence happens inside a single snapshot transaction; on any failure
the transaction is discarded and the running system is left unchanged.
.PP
It is surfaced as a \f[B]zypper\f[R] subcommand (an executable in
\f[I]/usr/lib/zypper/commands\f[R]) and is also invokable directly.
.PP
Options are \f[B]key=value\f[R] pairs and may precede or follow the
verb.
POSIX \f[B]\[en]flag\f[R] style is not used for options; the only
tolerated flag aliases are \f[B]\[en]version\f[R], \f[B]\[en]help\f[R],
and \f[B]-h\f[R].
Behaviour is never controlled via environment variables.
.SH VERBS
.TP
\f[B]apply\f[R]
Converge the system to the desired manifest inside one snapshot
transaction, recording what was applied.
Idempotent: a second run against an unchanged manifest and an undrifted
system makes no changes.
.TP
\f[B]diff\f[R]
Dry run.
Print what \f[B]apply\f[R] would change without modifying the system or
opening a transaction.
Drift is computed against the desired manifest.
.TP
\f[B]verify\f[R]
Check whether the actual state equals a reference declaration (the
applied record by default, or a supplied manifest), modulo the
keep-list.
.TP
\f[B]status\f[R]
Print the current declarative state: applied manifest hash, generation,
and a one-line drift summary.
Read-only and fast.
.TP
\f[B]describe\f[R]
Read the actual state of the declarable scopes and emit it in the
selected serialisation (JSON by default, YAML on request).
.TP
\f[B]init\f[R]
Onboard a machine: adopt its current state as the managed baseline in
one command.
Reads the live system, opens a snapshot, writes the described state as
the applied record, and converges nothing.
.TP
\f[B]version\f[R]
Print the program name, version, and embedded spec hash.
Exit 0.
.TP
\f[B]help\f[R]
Print usage.
Exit 0.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]mode=auto|external|internal\f[R]
Transaction binding.
Default \f[I]auto\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]manifest-path=\f[R]<path>
Desired manifest (apply, diff); reference manifest for verify.
.TP
\f[B]format=json|yaml\f[R]
Serialisation for this invocation\[cq]s manifest I/O.
When omitted, the operative file extension decides, else the
\f[B]manifest-format\f[R] default.
.TP
\f[B]state-path=\f[R]<path>
Captured actual state for verify and diff (offline; default reads live).
.TP
\f[B]root=\f[R]<path>
Root to describe.
Default \f[I]/\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]out=\f[R]<path>
describe output file.
Default stdout.
.TP
\f[B]on-unreadable=error|warn\f[R]
How a live-state read treats a source it cannot read.
\f[I]error\f[R] (default) fails the run naming the source;
\f[I]warn\f[R] omits the affected items with a diagnostic and continues.
Accepted by \f[B]describe\f[R], \f[B]diff\f[R], \f[B]verify\f[R], and
\f[B]apply\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]scope=etc|full\f[R]
Read scope for \f[B]describe\f[R] and \f[B]verify\f[R].
\f[I]etc\f[R] (default) inspects only \f[I]/etc\f[R]; \f[I]full\f[R]
additionally audits \f[I]/usr\f[R] and \f[I]/boot\f[R] (expensive,
opt-in).
.PP
Other CONFIG knobs accepted as key=value options:
\f[B]manifest-format\f[R], \f[B]repo-lock\f[R], \f[B]content-store\f[R],
\f[B]keep-list\f[R], \f[B]signature-verification\f[R],
\f[B]keyring\f[R], \f[B]activation-policy\f[R], \f[B]applied-root\f[R].
A command-line option overrides the corresponding preset value.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success (converged, no-op, system matches declaration, or describe
emitted).
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
Logical failure (convergence failed and discarded; verify found drift;
manifest invalid, unsafe-YAML, or unverified; state collection failed).
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
Invocation error (bad arguments; unknown format value; manifest
unreadable; insufficient privilege; transaction mechanism unavailable;
output path unwritable; malformed state dump).
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]/usr/lib/zypper-declarative/applied.json\f[R]
The applied record of the current generation, stored inside the
snapshot.
.TP
\f[I]/etc/etc.syncpoint\f[R]
Never written to or deleted by the file converger.
.SH INSTALLATION
.PP
Install from the openSUSE Build Service:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
zypper install zypper-declarative
\f[R]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\f[B]zypper\f[R](8), \f[B]snapper\f[R](8),
\f[B]transactional-update\f[R](8)
